    <h1 class="h1" style="max-width:900px">KVKK-compliant websites: privacy notice, cookie policy and explicit consent.</h1>
    <div class="bi-meta"><div class="bi-tag"><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 12V4a1 1 0 0 1 1-1h8l9 9-9 9-9-9z"/><circle cx="8" cy="8" r="1.5"/></svg>Security</div><div><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="3" y="5" width="18" height="16" rx="2"/><path d="M3 10h18M8 3v4M16 3v4"/></svg><time datetime="2026-10-31">31 October 2026</time></div><div><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/><path d="M12 7v5l3 2"/></svg>9 min read</div></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section-tight" style="padding-top:0">
  <div class="wrap bi-grid">
    <article class="article">
      <div class="article-img"><img src="../assets/img/blog/kvkk-uyumlu-web-sitesi.svg" alt=""></div>
      <p class="bi-lead">If your site has a contact form, a Google Analytics code or a WhatsApp button, you are processing personal data. A <b>KVKK-compliant website</b> is no longer a luxury reserved for large companies; a small business’s site is subject to the same rules.</p>
      <p>In July 2025 the Personal Data Protection Authority published the updated version of its <b>Guidelines on Cookie Practices</b>; in 2024, important amendments to Law No. 6698 on special categories of data and cross-border transfers came into force. In this article we explain step by step where a website carries KVKK risk, the difference between a privacy notice and explicit consent, what a proper cookie banner should look like and what you need to fix on the technical side.</p>
      <div class="bi-note"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 3l8 3v6c0 4.5-3.4 8.3-8 9-4.6-.7-8-4.5-8-9V6l8-3z"/><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"/></svg><div><b>Important note:</b> This article is for general information only and is not legal advice. We recommend working with a KVKK expert or lawyer on your site’s data processing activities.</div></div>
      <h2 id="kapsam" class="bi-h2" data-n="01">What personal data does a website process?</h2>
      <p>The first step toward KVKK compliance is honestly listing where your site collects data. Most businesses only think of the contact form, yet personal data is processed in far more places than you might think:</p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Forms:</b> contact, quote, appointment, job application, newsletter sign-up. Names, emails, phone numbers and even extensive data such as CVs.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Cookies and tracking codes:</b> Google Analytics, Meta pixel, Google Ads conversion tag, heatmap tools. These collect online identifiers and behavioural data.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Server logs:</b> IP address, browser information, access time. Traffic logs kept under Law No. 5651 also fall into this group.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Third-party plugins:</b> embedded YouTube videos, Google Maps, live chat and WhatsApp widgets, social media share buttons.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Membership and e-commerce:</b> account details, addresses, order history. Payment card data is usually processed directly by the payment institution.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p>Once you have this list, answer three questions for each piece of data: <b>Why</b> am I collecting it, <b>which legal basis</b> am I relying on, and <b>how long</b> do I keep it? These answers form the backbone of the privacy notice and cookie policy we’ll discuss shortly.</p>
      <div class="bi-note"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M13 2L4 14h7l-1 8 9-12h-7l1-8z"/></svg><div><b>Quick tip:</b> Removing a data field you don’t use from a form is the cheapest KVKK measure. There is usually no reason to ask for a national ID number or date of birth on a quote form; data minimisation is one of the law’s core principles.</div></div>
      <h2 id="aydinlatma" class="bi-h2" data-n="02">A privacy notice and explicit consent are not the same thing</h2>
      <p>The most common mistake on websites is combining the privacy notice and explicit consent into a single checkbox. The phrase “I have read and accept the KVKK text” mixes up two separate obligations.</p>
      <h3>Privacy notice: mandatory in every case</h3>
      <p>Under Article 10 of the law, you must inform people when collecting data: the identity of the data controller, the purposes of processing, to whom and for what purpose the data may be transferred, the method of collection and legal basis, and the person’s rights under Article 11. The notice is <b>information</b>; it does not ask for consent. So instead of an “I have read and accept” box, a clearly visible link right below the form is enough.</p>
      <h3>Explicit consent: only when there is no other basis</h3>
      <p>Explicit consent is consent relating to a specific matter, based on information and given freely. The data controller should first check whether another processing condition applies, such as entering into a contract, a legal obligation or legitimate interest; only if none applies should it rely on explicit consent. For example, replying to someone who filled in a quote form doesn’t require explicit consent, but sending that same person campaign emails requires separate consent.</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Privacy notice</th><th>Explicit consent</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Replying to a contact form</td><td>Required</td><td>Usually not required</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Newsletter and campaign emails</td><td>Required</td><td>Separate consent required (along with İYS registration)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Strictly necessary cookies (session, cart, security)</td><td>Required</td><td>Not required</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Advertising and remarketing cookies</td><td>Required</td><td>Required</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Special category data, such as health information in a job application</td><td>Required</td><td>Depends; conditions were broadened by the 2024 amendment</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>Explicit consent cannot be made a precondition of the service. If someone who doesn’t consent to the newsletter can’t submit the contact form, the consent is no longer freely given. Checkboxes must also be <b>unticked</b> by default.</p>
      <h2 id="cerez" class="bi-h2" data-n="03">The right cookie banner according to KVKK’s cookie guidelines</h2>
      <p>KVKK’s July 2025 <b>Guidelines on Cookie Practices</b> are the most concrete roadmap for website owners. The guidelines’ core message is this: not every cookie requires explicit consent, but cookies that do require consent <b>must not run</b> until the user agrees.</p>
      <h3>Which cookies can be used without consent?</h3>
      <p>The guidelines treat examples such as session and form input cookies, authentication, user-centric security, load balancing, interface preferences like language, and shopping cart cookies as falling under processing conditions other than explicit consent. They state that first-party analytics cookies may also be considered within this scope, provided they are limited to measuring how the site works, the data is minimised through measures such as IP masking, and there is no cross-site tracking. Advertising, remarketing and third-party tracking cookies, however, require explicit consent.</p>
      <h3>What should a good cookie management panel look like?</h3>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Equal buttons:</b> The guidelines cite presenting the “Accept”, “Reject” and “Preferences” buttons in the panel shown on entry with equal colour, size and font as good practice. Burying the reject option in a small grey link goes against this principle.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Off by default:</b> Cookie categories based on explicit consent should be inactive at first on the preferences screen.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Consent first, code second:</b> Advertising pixels and tracking tags should not load until the user consents. Just showing a banner while running everything in the background is not compliance.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Withdrawable consent:</b> Users should be able to change their preference easily later; the guidelines cite a small icon or banner in a corner of the page as a good example.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>No cookie walls:</b> “Cookie wall” practices that make access to the site conditional on accepting all cookies can undermine free will and may not count as valid consent.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Consent fatigue:</b> Instead of asking again on every visit, it is recommended to re-prompt at intervals proportionate to the cookie’s lifetime.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p>The cookie policy, on the other hand, is a separate page linked from the panel: it lists the cookies used, their purpose, duration, whether they are first- or third-party and how to manage them. The guidelines also stress that phrases such as “by using this site you are deemed to accept cookies” do not constitute valid consent.</p>
      <blockquote><div class="bi-q"><svg class="ic" width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 7H5a1 1 0 0 0-1 1v4a1 1 0 0 0 1 1h3v1a3 3 0 0 1-3 3M19 7h-4a1 1 0 0 0-1 1v4a1 1 0 0 0 1 1h3v1a3 3 0 0 1-3 3"/></svg></div>A cookie banner isn’t decoration; it’s a key: the door shouldn’t open until the user says “yes”.</blockquote>
      <h2 id="formlar" class="bi-h2" data-n="04">Contact forms, newsletters and commercial message permissions</h2>
      <p>Forms are the most visible part of KVKK-compliant website design, and when set up properly they don’t harm the user experience. The structure we recommend is as follows:</p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Step 1 —</b> Right below the form, add a short sentence such as “Your personal data is processed in accordance with our Privacy Notice.” with a link to the text. A checkbox is not required.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Step 2 —</b> If you need marketing permission, add a separate, optional box that is unticked by default. Submitting the form should not depend on this box.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Step 3 —</b> If you want to send commercial electronic messages, don’t forget the obligations under Law No. 6563 and the Message Management System (İYS); consents obtained must be registered in İYS.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Step 4 —</b> Determine where form data goes: an email inbox, a CRM, Google Sheets or a form tool hosted abroad. This directly concerns the cross-border transfer issue we discuss below.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Step 5 —</b> Set a retention period. If there is no reason to keep form entries from two years ago that never turned into a deal indefinitely, delete or anonymise them periodically.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p>You should also offer a way for people to exercise their rights under Article 11 of KVKK. Requests must be answered within 30 days at the latest; an application form or a clear email address on the site makes this process easier.</p>
      <h2 id="yurtdisi" class="bi-h2" data-n="05">The 2024 amendments: cross-border transfers and foreign services</h2>
      <p>The amendments made by Law No. 7499, which came into force on 1 June 2024, are especially important for websites when it comes to <b>transferring data abroad</b>. If you use hosting, an email service, an analytics tool, a form service or a CRM hosted abroad, your data may be transferred abroad.</p>
      <p>Under the new regime, a transfer can be made if a processing condition exists and the data goes to a country with an adequacy decision, or one of the appropriate safeguards is in place, such as the <b>standard contract</b> published by the Authority. A standard contract must be notified to the Authority within five business days of signing. The old option of transferring on the basis of explicit consent ceased to be a stand-alone basis after the transition period ended on 1 September 2024; explicit consent remains only as a narrow exception for occasional transfers.</p>
      <div class="bi-note"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M13 2L4 14h7l-1 8 9-12h-7l1-8z"/></svg><div><b>Practical tip:</b> Make a list of all the third-party services you use on your site (hosting, email, analytics, forms, live chat) and note in which country each processes data. Choosing infrastructure hosted in Türkiye where possible reduces the compliance burden.</div></div>
      <p>The same amendment also broadened the conditions for processing special categories of data. Sites that ask for health information in a job application form, or that design an appointment form for a clinic, should assess this separately, because additional security measures set by the Board also apply to special category data.</p>
      <h2 id="teknik" class="bi-h2" data-n="06">Technical and administrative security measures for KVKK</h2>
      <p>Article 12 of KVKK obliges the data controller to take appropriate technical and administrative measures to protect data against unlawful access. For a website, this translates into concrete, measurable steps:</p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>HTTPS on every page:</b> A valid SSL certificate and a permanent redirect from HTTP to HTTPS. For details, see <a href="web-sitesi-guvenligi">our website security and SSL guide</a>.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Up-to-date software:</b> Regularly updating your CMS, plugins and libraries; removing unused plugins.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Access management:</b> Strong passwords and two-factor authentication for the admin panel; only the people who need it can access form entries.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Backups and logging:</b> Regular, encrypted backups and logging of panel access.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Data breach plan:</b> When a breach is detected, the Board must be notified as soon as possible and within 72 hours at the latest. Writing down in advance who does what saves time in a crisis.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p>These measures matter not only for KVKK but also for your site’s reputation and search visibility. A compromised site can lose both customer trust and its Google rankings.</p>
      <h2 id="verbis" class="bi-h2" data-n="07">Is VERBİS registration required, and how large are the fines?</h2>
      <p>VERBİS (the Data Controllers’ Registry Information System) is not mandatory for every business. According to Board decisions, data controllers whose main activity is not processing special category data, with fewer than 50 employees per year and an annual balance sheet total below 100 million TL, are exempt from registration. With the Authority’s announcement of 1 October 2025, those with fewer than 10 employees and a balance sheet below 10 million TL are also exempt, even if their main activity is processing special category data.</p>
      <div class="bi-note"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 3l8 3v6c0 4.5-3.4 8.3-8 9-4.6-.7-8-4.5-8-9V6l8-3z"/><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"/></svg><div><b>Warning:</b> The VERBİS exemption only removes the registration obligation. The obligations to inform, keep data secure and respond to requests apply to every business; a small company’s site must comply with these rules too.</div></div>
      <p>Administrative fines are updated every year by the revaluation rate, and the Authority publishes the current table on its website. For 2026, the lower limit for breaching the obligation to inform is around 85,000 TL and the upper limit around 1.7 million TL; for data security and VERBİS violations the upper limit exceeds 17 million TL. We recommend checking the Authority’s official table for the exact figures.</p>
      <div class="bi-check">
        <div class="eyebrow">// KVKK COMPLIANCE CHECKLIST</div>
        <ul class="bi-list"><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Have all data collection points on the site been listed?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Is there a privacy notice link below every form?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Is marketing consent a separate, optional, unticked box?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Do Accept / Reject / Preferences look equal in the cookie panel?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Are advertising and tracking codes blocked until consent is given?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Have services abroad and the transfer basis been identified?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Are a request channel and a data breach response plan ready?</span></li></ul>
      </div>
      <h2 id="sss" class="bi-h2" data-n="08">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="bi-faq"><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>Is KVKK mandatory for a small business’s website?</summary><p>Yes. KVKK applies to all individuals and legal entities that process personal data. Small businesses may be exempt from VERBİS registration, but the obligations to inform, keep data secure and respond to data subject requests still apply.</p></details><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>Do I need explicit consent for a contact form?</summary><p>Usually not. Processing data to respond to a request is generally considered preparation for entering into a contract or a legitimate interest; a privacy notice is enough. For marketing communications, however, separate and optional consent is required.</p></details><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>Do I need cookie consent to use Google Analytics?</summary><p>KVKK’s cookie guidelines may treat first-party analytics that only measures how the site works, with minimised data and no cross-site tracking, as falling under conditions other than explicit consent. Setups involving advertising features, remarketing or third-party tracking, however, require explicit consent.</p></details><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>Does a cookie banner have to have a Reject button?</summary><p>The Authority’s 2025 guidelines cite presenting the Accept, Reject and Preferences buttons in equal size, colour and font as good practice. Making rejecting as easy as accepting is the safest way to show that consent is based on free will.</p></details><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>Can a KVKK privacy notice be copied from a template?</summary><p>Not recommended. A privacy notice must describe what data you actually process, for what purpose and with which services. Copying another company’s text can lead to inaccurate and misleading information.</p></details></div>
      <h2 id="sonuc">Final word</h2>
      <p>A KVKK-compliant website is not a site full of long legal texts; it is a site that knows what it collects, doesn’t collect what it doesn’t need, offers users a real choice and stores data securely. This approach also increases visitors’ trust. We recommend working with an expert on the content of legal texts; on the technical side, such as the cookie panel, form flows, loading tags based on consent and server security, we are by your side with <a href="web-cozumleri">our web solutions</a>. If you are planning a new site, you can also <a href="kurumsal-web-sitesi-rehberi">read our corporate website guide</a>, and for a technical KVKK check of your current site, <a href="iletisim">get in touch with us</a>.</p>
